@@ -115,9 +115,20 @@ Environment variables:
 | `BUILDER=docker\|podman` | Container runtime. Podman is preferred when both are present, except on Apple silicon. |
 | `REBUILD_IMAGE=1` | Rebuilds the image even when its tag exists. |
 | `PCSX2=...` | Emulator command for `kz-pcsx2`. |
+| `KZ_PERSIST=1` | Starts this checkout's persistent container when it is missing or runs another image, then runs commands in it. |
 
 Inside the container the driver also reads `KZ_PROJECT_ROOT`, `KZ_HOST_OS` and `KZ_RUN_OUT` (the descriptor path).
 
+### Persistent container and linked entries
+
+A container start costs seconds on Docker Desktop, so `kazarov up` starts one detached container for this checkout, running `sleep infinity` from the same image with the same mounts. Later `build`, `run` and `shell` commands `docker exec` into it when it runs the current image tag. A stale container (another tag) is not used, and the driver says so; `kazarov up` replaces it. `kazarov down` removes it. The name is `kz-<project>-<10 hex digits of the sha256 of the project root>`, so the Windows script finds the same container.
+
+- `kazarov up`: starts the container, or does nothing if it already runs the current tag.
+- `kazarov down`: removes it.
+- `KZ_PERSIST=1`: the driver starts it on first use, and replaces a stale one. Export it once to use the persistent container for every command.
+
+The container sees the project root at `/work/$KZ_PROJECT`. Each top-level symlink or junction in the root whose target is a directory outside the root is mounted at the same name under `/work`, so a worktree's `data` junction works inside the container. The mounts are fixed when the container starts: after adding or removing a linked entry, run `kazarov down` and then `kazarov up`.
+
 ## Helper tools
 
 All are Python 3 with the standard library, except `kazarov` and `kz-pcsx2`, which are bash.
